1
00:00:00,667 --> 00:00:03,167
In January seventeen forty-six,

2
00:00:03,187 --> 00:00:06,567
a professor in the Dutch city of Leiden held a glass jar

3
00:00:06,587 --> 00:00:10,097
of water in one hand and touched a wire with the other.

4
00:00:10,507 --> 00:00:12,457
The shock threw him back.

5
00:00:12,587 --> 00:00:15,527
He wrote to a friend that he would not take a second one

6
00:00:15,547 --> 00:00:17,817
for the whole kingdom of France.

7
00:00:17,947 --> 00:00:21,617
Pieter van Musschenbroek had stumbled onto something new.

8
00:00:21,787 --> 00:00:24,727
So had a German cleric, Ewald von Kleist,

9
00:00:24,747 --> 00:00:25,937
a few months earlier.

10
00:00:26,347 --> 00:00:29,767
For the first time, electricity could be collected,

11
00:00:29,787 --> 00:00:32,377
kept, and released all at once.

12
00:00:32,507 --> 00:00:35,097
Within months it was a sensation.

13
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:38,727
In Paris, the Abbé Nollet lined up a hundred and eighty

14
00:00:38,747 --> 00:00:41,447
soldiers of the royal guard, hand in hand,

15
00:00:41,467 --> 00:00:43,937
and discharged a jar through all of them.

16
00:00:44,267 --> 00:00:45,857
They jumped as one.

17
00:00:46,187 --> 00:00:49,057
The Leyden jar was the first capacitor.

18
00:00:49,147 --> 00:00:52,567
And the idea inside it is in every circuit you will ever

19
00:00:52,587 --> 00:00:53,377
build.

20
00:00:54,733 --> 00:00:57,433
Take the two metal plates from the last video,

21
00:00:57,453 --> 00:00:59,673
facing each other across a gap,

22
00:00:59,693 --> 00:01:01,923
and connect a battery between them.

23
00:01:02,093 --> 00:01:04,363
The battery does not create charge.

24
00:01:04,413 --> 00:01:05,523
It moves it.

25
00:01:05,933 --> 00:01:08,273
Electrons are pulled off one plate,

26
00:01:08,293 --> 00:01:11,363
pushed through the wire, and piled onto the other.

27
00:01:11,533 --> 00:01:13,363
One plate is left positive.

28
00:01:13,613 --> 00:01:17,043
The other becomes negative by exactly the same amount.

29
00:01:17,453 --> 00:01:20,873
As the charge builds, so does the field in the gap,

30
00:01:20,893 --> 00:01:23,883
and with it a voltage between the plates.

31
00:01:24,013 --> 00:01:26,883
That voltage pushes back against the battery.

32
00:01:26,973 --> 00:01:29,313
When it equals the battery's own voltage,

33
00:01:29,333 --> 00:01:30,483
the flow stops.

34
00:01:30,893 --> 00:01:32,763
Now disconnect the battery.

35
00:01:32,893 --> 00:01:34,123
Nothing changes.

36
00:01:34,173 --> 00:01:36,233
The charges stay where they are,

37
00:01:36,253 --> 00:01:39,313
each plate holding the other's charge in place across

38
00:01:39,333 --> 00:01:40,163
the gap.

39
00:01:40,253 --> 00:01:42,323
Electricity, stored.

40
00:01:43,667 --> 00:01:46,937
How much charge does a given pair of plates hold?

41
00:01:47,107 --> 00:01:49,057
That depends on the voltage.

42
00:01:49,187 --> 00:01:52,177
Double the voltage, and you get double the charge.

43
00:01:52,307 --> 00:01:54,807
The ratio between them never changes,

44
00:01:54,827 --> 00:01:58,017
and that ratio is what we call capacitance.

45
00:01:58,147 --> 00:02:01,727
Capacitance is the charge stored for each volt.

46
00:02:01,747 --> 00:02:04,177
C equals Q over V.

47
00:02:04,467 --> 00:02:05,887
Think of a bucket.

48
00:02:05,907 --> 00:02:08,287
The voltage is the depth of the water,

49
00:02:08,307 --> 00:02:10,367
and the charge is the amount.

50
00:02:10,387 --> 00:02:14,047
A wide bucket holds far more water at the same depth than

51
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:15,287
a narrow one.

52
00:02:15,307 --> 00:02:17,927
Capacitance is the width of the bucket.

53
00:02:17,947 --> 00:02:21,567
Its unit is the farad, named after Michael Faraday:

54
00:02:21,587 --> 00:02:23,727
one coulomb for each volt.

55
00:02:23,747 --> 00:02:26,447
It was made official in eighteen eighty-one.

56
00:02:26,467 --> 00:02:29,887
Until then, every laboratory measured electricity in its

57
00:02:29,907 --> 00:02:30,967
own way.

58
00:02:30,987 --> 00:02:34,127
That year, the first International Electrical Congress

59
00:02:34,147 --> 00:02:37,807
met in Paris, and agreed on one set of units for the whole

60
00:02:37,827 --> 00:02:41,367
world: the ohm, the volt, the ampere,

61
00:02:41,387 --> 00:02:43,457
the coulomb, and the farad.

62
00:02:43,587 --> 00:02:46,057
And one farad is enormous.

63
00:02:46,307 --> 00:02:49,447
The capacitors on a circuit board are measured in millionths

64
00:02:49,467 --> 00:02:52,457
of a farad, or billionths, or less.

65
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:56,790
What decides the capacitance of two plates?

66
00:02:56,920 --> 00:02:59,870
Only their shape, and what lies between them.

67
00:03:00,280 --> 00:03:03,500
Make the plates larger, and there is more room for charge

68
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:04,820
to spread out.

69
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:08,150
Double the area, and you double the capacitance.

70
00:03:08,280 --> 00:03:12,150
Bring the plates closer, and something subtler happens.

71
00:03:12,280 --> 00:03:15,780
Remember that the voltage is the field multiplied by the

72
00:03:15,800 --> 00:03:16,510
gap.

73
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:20,100
With a smaller gap, the same charge produces a smaller

74
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:21,070
voltage.

75
00:03:21,160 --> 00:03:24,510
So for the same voltage, you can store more charge.

76
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,790
Halve the gap, and the capacitance doubles.

77
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:30,980
So capacitance equals a constant,

78
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:33,390
times the area, divided by the gap.

79
00:03:33,640 --> 00:03:37,100
The constant is called the permittivity of free space,

80
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:38,830
and it is very small.

81
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:39,710
How small?

82
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:42,780
Take two plates, each one metre square,

83
00:03:42,800 --> 00:03:44,310
a millimetre apart.

84
00:03:44,360 --> 00:03:48,230
Their capacitance is less than nine billionths of a farad.

85
00:03:48,280 --> 00:03:50,660
To reach one farad with that gap,

86
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,540
the plates would have to cover more than a hundred square

87
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:56,430
kilometres: the area of a city.

88
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:00,677
So how do real capacitors manage it?

89
00:04:00,807 --> 00:04:04,827
The answer was found by Faraday in eighteen thirty-seven.

90
00:04:04,847 --> 00:04:08,027
He filled the gap with different insulating materials,

91
00:04:08,047 --> 00:04:10,707
and found that the capacitance went up,

92
00:04:10,727 --> 00:04:13,597
by an amount that depended on the material.

93
00:04:13,767 --> 00:04:16,637
An insulator has no free electrons.

94
00:04:16,727 --> 00:04:19,917
But its molecules can stretch, or turn.

95
00:04:20,087 --> 00:04:23,427
In a field, their positive and negative sides shift a

96
00:04:23,447 --> 00:04:25,517
little in opposite directions.

97
00:04:25,687 --> 00:04:28,787
Each molecule becomes a tiny pair of charges,

98
00:04:28,807 --> 00:04:31,667
and together they produce a field of their own,

99
00:04:31,687 --> 00:04:32,877
pointing the other way.

100
00:04:33,287 --> 00:04:35,437
That weakens the field in the gap.

101
00:04:35,767 --> 00:04:39,357
A weaker field means a lower voltage for the same charge.

102
00:04:39,607 --> 00:04:42,227
And a lower voltage for the same charge is,

103
00:04:42,247 --> 00:04:44,877
by definition, a larger capacitance.

104
00:04:45,207 --> 00:04:47,757
We call such a material a dielectric.

105
00:04:48,167 --> 00:04:51,117
Paper multiplies the capacitance by about three.

106
00:04:51,447 --> 00:04:53,277
Glass, by five to ten.

107
00:04:53,607 --> 00:04:55,757
Some ceramics, by thousands.

108
00:04:56,167 --> 00:04:58,877
A dielectric does one more useful thing.

109
00:04:58,967 --> 00:05:02,547
It can stand a far stronger field than air before it breaks

110
00:05:02,567 --> 00:05:05,917
down, so the plates can sit much closer together.

111
00:05:06,327 --> 00:05:09,347
A modern capacitor is thin metal foil,

112
00:05:09,367 --> 00:05:12,467
a film a few thousandths of a millimetre thick,

113
00:05:12,487 --> 00:05:15,667
and metres of it rolled into a cylinder the size of a

114
00:05:15,687 --> 00:05:16,637
fingertip.

115
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,590
Charging a capacitor takes work.

116
00:05:20,720 --> 00:05:23,390
The first bit of charge crosses easily.

117
00:05:23,520 --> 00:05:27,190
But each one after it must be pushed against the voltage

118
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:28,350
already there.

119
00:05:28,480 --> 00:05:30,140
The work is not lost.

120
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:31,190
It is stored.

121
00:05:31,600 --> 00:05:35,260
Because the voltage starts at zero and climbs steadily,

122
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:38,230
the average push is half the final voltage.

123
00:05:38,640 --> 00:05:41,420
So the energy stored is one half,

124
00:05:41,440 --> 00:05:44,550
times the capacitance, times the voltage squared.

125
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:46,230
Where is that energy?

126
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:47,510
Not in the metal.

127
00:05:47,840 --> 00:05:50,820
Maxwell showed that it is in the field itself,

128
00:05:50,840 --> 00:05:52,990
in the space between the plates.

129
00:05:53,120 --> 00:05:55,590
Empty space, holding energy.

130
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:58,070
And it can come back very fast.

131
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:01,540
A camera flash takes seconds to charge and a thousandth

132
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:02,950
of a second to fire.

133
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:06,300
A defibrillator charges for a few seconds,

134
00:06:06,320 --> 00:06:09,340
then delivers a couple of hundred joules to a heart in

135
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:11,510
a few thousandths of a second.

136
00:06:12,867 --> 00:06:15,497
A capacitor never charges in an instant,

137
00:06:15,667 --> 00:06:18,457
because every circuit has some resistance.

138
00:06:18,867 --> 00:06:22,367
At first the current is large, and the voltage climbs

139
00:06:22,387 --> 00:06:23,097
quickly.

140
00:06:23,507 --> 00:06:27,087
As the capacitor fills, the current fades and the climb

141
00:06:27,107 --> 00:06:28,137
slows.

142
00:06:28,307 --> 00:06:32,287
The pace is set by one number: the resistance multiplied

143
00:06:32,307 --> 00:06:33,577
by the capacitance.

144
00:06:33,907 --> 00:06:35,977
It is called the time constant.

145
00:06:36,467 --> 00:06:40,047
After one time constant the capacitor is about two thirds

146
00:06:40,067 --> 00:06:40,617
full.

147
00:06:40,947 --> 00:06:44,857
After five, it is full for every practical purpose.

148
00:06:45,107 --> 00:06:46,377
Notice something else.

149
00:06:46,787 --> 00:06:49,577
Once it is charged, the current stops.

150
00:06:49,667 --> 00:06:53,407
A steady voltage cannot drive a current through a capacitor:

151
00:06:53,427 --> 00:06:54,777
there is a gap in the way.

152
00:06:55,187 --> 00:06:58,487
But a changing voltage keeps charge flowing on and off

153
00:06:58,507 --> 00:07:00,057
the plates all the time.

154
00:07:00,387 --> 00:07:04,047
A capacitor blocks direct current and passes alternating

155
00:07:04,067 --> 00:07:04,617
current.

156
00:07:04,947 --> 00:07:07,767
And when you connect two of them side by side,

157
00:07:07,787 --> 00:07:10,857
their areas add, so their capacitances add.

158
00:07:11,267 --> 00:07:14,687
Connect them end to end, and the gaps add instead,

159
00:07:14,707 --> 00:07:17,417
so the total is smaller than either one.

160
00:07:18,767 --> 00:07:22,747
Franklin wired several Leyden jars together to store more,

161
00:07:22,767 --> 00:07:24,677
and called the set a battery.

162
00:07:25,087 --> 00:07:26,757
That is where the word comes from.

163
00:07:27,247 --> 00:07:29,667
Volta, in seventeen eighty-two,

164
00:07:29,687 --> 00:07:33,067
called the device a condenser, a name you will still find

165
00:07:33,087 --> 00:07:34,437
on old drawings.

166
00:07:34,527 --> 00:07:36,357
Today they are everywhere.

167
00:07:36,447 --> 00:07:39,997
Capacitors smooth the output of every power supply.

168
00:07:40,127 --> 00:07:43,427
Great banks of them stand in substations to correct the

169
00:07:43,447 --> 00:07:44,587
power factor.

170
00:07:44,607 --> 00:07:48,374
The glass of a touch screen hides a grid of tiny capacitors,

171
00:07:48,394 --> 00:07:51,227
and your finger changes them as it comes near.

172
00:07:51,247 --> 00:07:54,667
And capacitance appears where nobody asked for it.

173
00:07:54,687 --> 00:07:58,507
Any two conductors with a gap between them form a capacitor.

174
00:07:58,527 --> 00:08:02,427
A long underground cable, a core and a sheath with insulation

175
00:08:02,447 --> 00:08:06,054
between, has so much of it that simply energising the

176
00:08:06,074 --> 00:08:08,357
cable draws a charging current.

177
00:08:08,407 --> 00:08:10,277
Engineers have to design for it.

178
00:08:11,733 --> 00:08:13,323
So let's put it together.

179
00:08:13,573 --> 00:08:17,403
A capacitor is two conductors with a gap between them.

180
00:08:17,493 --> 00:08:20,513
A voltage moves charge from one to the other,

181
00:08:20,533 --> 00:08:21,883
and the charge stays.

182
00:08:22,053 --> 00:08:25,233
Capacitance is the charge stored for each volt,

183
00:08:25,253 --> 00:08:26,803
measured in farads.

184
00:08:26,853 --> 00:08:29,153
It grows with the area of the plates,

185
00:08:29,173 --> 00:08:32,963
shrinks with the gap, and is multiplied by a dielectric.

186
00:08:33,013 --> 00:08:35,113
The energy is stored in the field,

187
00:08:35,133 --> 00:08:38,083
and it equals one half C V squared.

188
00:08:38,133 --> 00:08:40,593
A capacitor takes time to charge,

189
00:08:40,613 --> 00:08:44,163
blocks a steady current, and passes a changing one.

190
00:08:44,293 --> 00:08:47,473
So far, every charge we have looked at has been standing

191
00:08:47,493 --> 00:08:49,323
still, or nearly.

192
00:08:49,493 --> 00:08:53,233
Next time, we set them moving, and find a second field

193
00:08:53,253 --> 00:08:55,003
that nobody expected.

194
00:08:55,093 --> 00:08:58,513
If this helped you see it, and not just calculate it,

195
00:08:58,533 --> 00:09:01,723
please like, share, and subscribe to this channel.
